**assistant**:
> `PyCall` is using its private Conda Python:
> 
> ```text
> ~/.julia/conda/3/lib/python3.9/
> ```
> 
> Its installed `pandas` binary was built against a different NumPy version. Reinstall NumPy and pandas *together* in that environment.
> 
> From a terminal:
> 
> ```bash
> ~/.julia/conda/3/bin/conda install -y --force-reinstall numpy pandas
> ```
> 
> Then quit and restart Julia, and test:
> 
> ```julia
> using PyCall
> pyimport("numpy")
> pyimport("pandas")
> pyimport("positions")
> ```
> 
> If Conda cannot resolve it, reset Julia’s private Conda environment:
> 
> ```bash
> rm -rf ~/.julia/conda/3
> ```
> 
> Then in Julia:
> 
> ```julia
> using Pkg
> Pkg.build("PyCall")
> ```
> 
> Reinstall any Python dependencies required by `positions.py` afterward.
> 
> To confirm which Python PyCall uses:
> 
> ```julia
> using PyCall
> println(PyCall.python)
> ```
> 
> Avoid installing/upgrading NumPy or pandas with `pip` inside `~/.julia/conda/3`; use Conda there, or instead configure PyCall to use a dedicated external virtual/Conda environment and rebuild `PyCall`.
